== Technical Implementation ==

=== Technology Stack ===

|=Component|=Technology
|Android language|Kotlin
|UI framework|Jetpack Compose
|Architecture pattern|MVVM with ViewModels, repositories/DAOs, services, and EventBus messaging
|Local database|SQLite with Room ORM, schema v31
|Android entities|17 entities
|Build system|Gradle 9.6.0 wrapper, Android Gradle Plugin 9.4.1, Kotlin 2.2.10; Kotlin DSL
|Maps in app|OpenStreetMap / OSMDroid
|Web maps|MapLibre / OpenStreetMap tiles
|Charts|Compose/Canvas and MPAndroidChart in Android; Chart.js on the web
|Connectivity|Bluetooth LE, WebSocket, REST API
|Server|Python WebSocket server plus Flask REST API
|Server database|PostgreSQL durable storage plus Redis live-history cache
|Deployment|Docker, nginx reverse proxy
|Minimum Android|Android 10 (API 29)
|Target Android|Android 14 (API 34)
|Compile SDK|Android API 36
|Java bytecode target|11

=== Codebase Overview ===

* Kotlin/Jetpack Compose Android app with ViewModels, DAOs/repositories, sensor services, WorkManager downloads, and media synchronization
* Python WebSocket service and Flask REST application
* Flask blueprints for sessions, planned events, media, analysis, heatmap, health, and errors
* Static Live, Analysis, Heatmap, and Route Rerun web dashboards
* Unit and regression coverage for recording recovery, timestamps, achievements, sport classification, laps, media location, retention, and synchronization

=== Android Database Schema (17 Entities) ===

StageGroup, User, Event, Metric, Location, Weather, DeviceStatus, CurrentRecording, LapTime, PlannedEvent, Clothing, WheelSprocket, Network, Waypoint, EventMedia, DisciplineTransition, WaypointPhoto.

Schema **v29** added structured sport metadata, **v30** added stage grouping, and **v31** added media coordinates. The database builder no longer uses destructive migration fallback: a missing migration fails instead of silently deleting existing recordings. Back up the database before upgrades.

=== Server Data Model ===

* Users with identity fields, birth date, and BMI
* Tracking sessions with start/end geocoding and structured sport-family, discipline, and event-format metadata
* GPS tracking points
* Lap times
* Heart rate devices
* Planned events with optional end date
* Session media with UUIDs, media type, file extension, thumbnails, caption, sort order, and optional latitude/longitude

== Build and Server Setup ==

=== Android Build ===

Clone [[GeoTracker on GitHub>>https://github.com/bernd-roth/GeoTracker]], open the project in Android Studio, and install Android SDK 36. Use the checked-in Gradle wrapper and a JDK compatible with the configured Android Gradle Plugin. From the project root on Windows:

{{code language="powershell"}}
.\gradlew.bat assembleDebug
.\gradlew.bat installDebug
{{/code}}

The APK output name is **geotracker.apk**. Configure location, notification, Bluetooth/sensor, and relevant media permissions on the phone. Complete the user profile, verify GPS reception, and configure the server address if live transfer is wanted.

=== Companion Services ===

Server files are under **app/src/main/java/at/co/netconsulting/geotracker/**: **websocket/** contains the WebSocket service, Compose configuration, nginx configuration, and web dashboard assets; **geotracker-flask/** contains the REST API.

The stack uses nginx for web pages and proxying, PostgreSQL for durable data, Redis for live history, the Python WebSocket process, and the Flask API. The standalone WebSocket listener uses **port 6789**. The checked-in Compose configuration publishes nginx on **80/443** and maps PostgreSQL **8021** to container port **5432**. These are repository settings, not a verification of the running deployment.

Before deploying, review the Compose file's host-specific volume paths, Flask build context, database configuration, Redis environment file, and external Redis network. The current Flask context refers to a host path and must match the deployed checkout. Configure POSTGRES_* and REDIS_* settings and DATA_RETENTION_HOURS as appropriate. Do not copy example credentials into production documentation.

From the websocket directory:

{{code language="bash"}}
docker compose up -d --build
{{/code}}

For a standalone WebSocket process, install **websockets**, **asyncpg**, **python-dateutil**, and **redis**, configure PostgreSQL/Redis connectivity, then run **python websocket_server.py**. Start/deploy the Flask API separately for REST-backed upload/download and historical analysis. Access the Live, Analysis, Heatmap, and Rerun pages through the configured nginx host. Deploy compatible Android, WebSocket, and Flask revisions together when using new metadata or media-location fields.

== System Requirements ==

* Android 10 or newer
* GPS/GNSS receiver
* Barometric pressure sensor recommended for improved altitude
* Bluetooth 4.0+ for heart rate monitors
* Camera/gallery permissions for media and workout-photo sharing
* Storage/media permissions for imports, exports, backups, and attachments
* Notification and exact-alarm permissions for reminders and long-running tracking
* Network access for WebSocket live sharing, REST API sync, weather forecasts, and third-party platform sync
* 2 GB RAM minimum, 4 GB+ recommended
* 100 MB+ free app storage, plus space for media, exports, and backups

